# Statistic and statistics graph cards

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Two Home Assistant cards show long-term statistics rather than the current state. The Statistic card gives one number for a period, such as the electricity used this month. The Statistics graph card draws several statistics as a chart over days or weeks. Both sit on the chart element that draws every Home Assistant chart.

This page owns these two cards and the chart element, and records how Civica treats charts. For the history of states, see [History graph card](history-graph-card.html). For the cards in the Energy panel, see [Energy cards](energy-cards.html). For recorded values in general, see [History, activity and statistics](../home-assistant/history-and-statistics.html).

This page covers:

- Statistic and statistics graph cards
  - `hui-statistic-card`
  - `hui-statistics-graph-card`
  - `ha-chart-base`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A **statistic card** has a name and icon in a header, then a large value with its unit. It has no heading element.

A **statistics graph card** has an optional title, a chart and a legend. The title is a first-level heading and holds a link to the History panel.

The **chart element** draws the plot, the legend of toggle buttons, a control to reset zoom and, where it can, a keyboard route through the values.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-statistic-card"><code>hui-statistic-card</code></h3>

A Lovelace card (`type: statistic`) that shows one statistic for one period. Three keys are required:

- `entity`: the statistic to show.
- `stat_type`: `mean`, `min`, `max` or `change`.
- `period`: the period to cover, as a calendar period, a fixed period or a rolling window.

```yaml
type: statistic
entity: sensor.hall_energy
stat_type: change
period:
  calendar:
    period: month
name: Electricity used this month
```

`name`, `icon`, `unit`, `footer` and `theme` change the look. A calendar period is a day, week, month or year. Its `offset` moves it back or forward, and the editor’s “last month” choice is the previous month. The card fetches its value when it loads and every five minutes. It shows a question mark when the value is null.

**What the Civica theme sets.** Square corners, a 1 pixel border, no shadow, and the theme’s text and icon colours. The name uses 19 pixel type in the secondary text colour. The value is 28 pixels at the standard text size, which is Home Assistant’s default because the theme does not set it.

**Keyboard.** The card can take focus, but nothing in it handles Enter or Space. A click or tap opens more information, and a keyboard user may not get the same result. Do not rely on the card as the only place for a figure. Show the same figure in an Entities card row or as text in a Markdown card.

**Say what the figure is.** The card does not state the statistic type or period. Put them in the name, for example “Electricity used this month”, rather than leaving them in the configuration.

<h3 id="child-hui-statistics-graph-card"><code>hui-statistics-graph-card</code></h3>

A Lovelace card (`type: statistics-graph`) that charts one or more statistics. `entities` is required and needs at least one entry, as a name or as an entry with `entity`, `name` and `color`.

```yaml
type: statistics-graph
title: Bedroom temperature, last 30 days
entities:
  - sensor.bedroom_temperature
days_to_show: 30
period: day
stat_types:
  - mean
  - max
chart_type: line
```

| Key | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | The card title, drawn as a first-level heading |
| `days_to_show` | How many days to chart; the default is 30 |
| `period` | `auto`, `5minute`, `hour`, `day`, `week` or `month` |
| `stat_types` | Any of `change`, `state`, `sum`, `min`, `max` and `mean`; the default is all six |
| `chart_type` | `line`, `bar`, `line-stack` or `bar-stack`; the default is line |
| `unit` | Replaces the unit |
| `min_y_axis`, `max_y_axis`, `fit_y_data` | Control the vertical range |
| `logarithmic_scale` | Uses a log scale |
| `hide_legend`, `expand_legend` | Hide the legend or open it in full |

The card refreshes every 60 minutes, or every five minutes when the period is `5minute`. If the data cannot be fetched, the chart shows no data and no error text.

**What the Civica theme sets.** Square corners, a 1 pixel border and no shadow, and the chart colours below.

**Headings.** The title is a first-level heading wherever the card sits. If your dashboard has its own heading order, leave the title off and put a heading in a [Markdown card](markdown-card.html) above the chart.

**No data table.** The native card has no table or text summary. The chart cannot be styled with CSS. The nearest accessible routes are a table written in a Markdown card (see [Data table](table.html)), a [History graph card](history-graph-card.html) and its data, or the View data disclosure in the Energy panel.

**What needs card-mod.** Changing the card’s own text style needs the card-mod integration. The chart itself is drawn on a canvas and cannot be restyled with it.

<h3 id="child-ha-chart-base"><code>ha-chart-base</code></h3>

The element that draws Home Assistant’s charts: statistics, history, Energy and the network, sankey and sunburst charts. It is not a card and has no YAML type, so a dashboard author cannot place it directly.

**What it adds.**

- A legend of real buttons: one that switches a series on or off, with a pressed state, and one that carries the series name.
- A “Reset zoom” button after zooming.
- A zoom slider on charts that allow it, and `--chart-max-height` for the maximum height, which is 350 pixels unless a theme sets it. The Civica theme does not.
- For bar, line, pie and scatter charts with at least two points, a keyboard route through the values labelled “Chart”. It loads when the chart first takes keyboard focus, not when a pointer does, and speaks in German, English, Spanish, French, Hmong and Italian, falling back to English. Charts of other kinds, such as timelines, sankey and network charts, have no such route and no label.
- A busy state while data loads.

**Colours.** Series take their colours from the theme’s chart colours in order. The Civica theme sets them, and some series share a colour, so a chart with many series cannot rely on colour alone. Name every series in the legend. The chart colours, and how they change in dark mode, are listed in [Chart colours](../styles/chart-colour.html).

<h2 id="energy-panel-charts">Energy panel charts</h2>

Inside the Energy panel, Civica’s presentation goes further than the theme. It applies to seven chart cards: power sources, energy usage, solar, gas, water, devices and device details. It changes only the line and bar series of those charts.

- **View data.** A disclosure after each chart holds a captioned table of every value supplied to the chart, including series hidden through the legend. Missing values show as missing, not as zero, and a value with no unit shows “Not supplied”. It pages at 100 rows with Previous and Next buttons and a count such as “Rows 1 to 100 of 250”.
- **Lines and columns.** Lines are 1.5 pixels wide. The plot is at least 280 pixels high. Columns are at most 75 pixels wide, or 55 pixels on narrow charts.
- **Legend.** It sits above the plot, and Home Assistant’s buttons to switch series on and off stay.
- **Reversible.** Choosing a theme that is not Civica removes the added presentation. Charts it cannot restyle stay as Home Assistant draws them.

The Statistic card, the Statistics graph card, History charts and third-party charts are not covered. They keep Home Assistant’s drawing, with the theme colours, and have no View data table. See [Energy and history](../patterns/energy.html) and [Compatibility and limits](../get-started/compatibility.html), and [Install Civica](../get-started/install.html) for adding the companion.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Both cards use Home Assistant’s own statistics, so figures match the rest of the product.
- The Statistic card gives a figure in one place, and the graph shows a trend.
- Neither card has a data table, and the Statistic card has no keyboard activation.
- The Statistics graph title is always a first-level heading.
- Charts need a text route for the same information. Hover text is extra and not enough alone.
- Failures to load are silent in the Statistics graph card.
- Zoom and roam are pointer gestures, and a keyboard route for them is not guaranteed.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

State the quantity, the unit, the kind of statistic (mean, minimum, maximum or change) and the period, in words. Keep power and energy on separate charts. Say plainly when there is no data. Keep to five series or fewer and give a text route to the values, such as a table. Do not draw a conclusion, such as a saving, that the data does not show. Use the six chart colours and no others.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: give each chart a text alternative or a table.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: name each series in the legend as well as by colour.
- 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: check each line colour against your card in light and dark mode.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: the title is a first-level heading, so check the heading order.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard: the Statistic card has no key handler.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the legend buttons report whether a series is shown.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text, 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.12 Text Spacing: check the legend and axis labels at 200%.
- 2.5.7 Dragging Movements: zoom and roam are pointer gestures.

For Energy charts, test light and dark modes, a 320 pixel width, keyboard use, larger text, legend and date changes, missing values, comparison periods and a return to the previous theme. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

